    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study applies PO, RMU and other unlearning methods as baselines for evaluating benign-input knowledge holes, but does not test recovery through the attacks in the claim.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports forgetting, general-utility and benign adjacent-input results, but no fine-tuning recovery attack, activation-direction ablation, quantization attack, or prompting-only recovery test.",
    "test_reason": "The study applies standard unlearning methods (KL-Min, NPO, PO, RMU) as baselines and proposes SPAR. It measures forget-set ASR, standard utility and collateral degradation on benign adjacent inputs ('knowledge holes'). No recovery attack is run: no relearning fine-tuning, direction ablation, quantization, or rephrasing/jailbreak/in-context recovery. Its central construct, collateral damage on benign inputs, is not any version's construct, so the rows bear on no version. SPAR is not evaluated against recovery attacks, so F is untested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right, but it understates the Qwen result. The authors conclude that standard MLLM unlearning methods systematically create knowledge holes, meaning severe degradation on benign adjacent inputs despite good ASR and standard utility. SPAR mitigates this on LLaVA-1.5-7B (Res. Q 7.07, 0% ASR). On Qwen2.5-VL-3B, SPAR does not unlearn at all (ASR 80.33%, above vanilla's 17%). The authors attribute this to the smaller hidden dimension and to refusal patterns being shielded as generic.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper evaluates multimodal unlearning baselines and introduces SPAR for protecting benign adjacent-input responses; it does not implement or test the recovery attacks in the claim.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Outcomes vary by model and unlearning method. The paper also reports SPAR knowledge-hole outcomes changing with retain weight and AFL filtering strength.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Baseline unlearning methods (PO, RMU, KL-Min on LLaVA; PO, RMU on Qwen) keep near-vanilla standard utility but sharply lower Res. Q on benign adjacent prompts (e.g., RMU 1.47 vs 7.17). PO also raises refusals to about 30%. KL-Min on Qwen shows no hole (7.13 vs 7.19). NPO collapses entirely, with utility at 0.",
        "proposed_claim": "Unlearning causes collateral degradation on benign inputs sharing generic patterns with the forget set, invisible to standard utility benchmarks; the size depends on method and model.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
          "r4",
